 # Model Comparison

-Models → Comparison compares generations and peers across the install's selectable provider inventory. Select models (search accepts comma-separated names and **Compare matching** selects the results), then toggle reasoning efforts. Compare GPT-6 Luna with GPT-5.6 Luna or Claude Opus in the same chart. Provider, model, effort, cost-axis and height preferences persist locally. **Fit visible** tightens axes around the selected points. The linear cost scale includes free endpoints; log scale explicitly excludes zero.
+Models → Comparison compares generations and peers across the install's selectable provider inventory. Select models (search accepts comma-separated names and **Compare matching** selects the results), then toggle reasoning efforts. Compare GPT-6 Luna with GPT-5.6 Luna or Claude Opus in the same chart. The intelligence index selector defaults to the newer v5.0.1 workload; choose v4.3.2 for its broader historical coverage. Index, provider, model, effort, cost-axis and height preferences persist locally. **Fit visible** tightens axes around the selected points. The linear cost scale includes free endpoints; log scale explicitly excludes zero.

 ## Data and methodology

 The server merges shipped public evidence with researched observations from `data/model-comparison.json`, taking the newer sourced metric for matching identities. PortOS-run task observations remain under Models → Performance. Reading Comparison performs no inference, online discovery, or provider calls; inventory comes from `getSelectableProviders()` and respects entitlement and enabled settings. The API exposes only provider id/name, model ids and supported effort ladders, never credentials or endpoints.

-PortOS intelligence index v1 is anchored to Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index **v4.3.2**, rather than selecting an unrelated benchmark in the UI. Exact results on that scale take priority. Other benchmark versions/families are mapped using least-squares calibration across at least three shared exact model/effort identities; inverted, degenerate, and high-error (>10 index points RMS) fits are rejected. A score outside the shared source-score range is a linear extrapolation only within 15% of that span, marked estimated in its method, and clamped to 0–100. Anything past that margin is not calibrated. The median of eligible calibrated results supplies an estimated index. Changing the visible selection never changes the reference population or normalization. This is an estimate of comparable intelligence, not a percent-correct score or a claim that every benchmark measures the same capability.
+PortOS intelligence index v1 is anchored to the selected Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index **v5.0.1** or **v4.3.2**. Exact results on that scale take priority. Other benchmark versions/families are mapped using least-squares calibration across at least three shared exact model/effort identities; inverted, degenerate, and high-error (>10 index points RMS) fits are rejected. A score outside the shared source-score range is a linear extrapolation only within 15% of that span, marked estimated in its method, and clamped to 0–100. Anything past that margin is not calibrated. The median of eligible calibrated results supplies an estimated index. Changing the visible selection never changes the reference population or normalization. This is an estimate of comparable intelligence, not a percent-correct score or a claim that every benchmark measures the same capability.

-Missing effort results interpolate between known efforts of the **same model** on an ordinal effort ladder. Boundary efforts borrow the nearest effort with low confidence and no invented gain. Explicit research estimates may use documented predecessor/peer evidence; their benchmark is `PortOS Research Index v1 (AA v4.3.2 scale)` and their methodology starts with `PortOS estimate:`. An unbenchmarked model in a recognized family receives a low-confidence median family baseline (same effort when available), with the reference range and constituent sources. Missing token prices may use the same family baseline. This does not assert a gain over a predecessor or a known endpoint price. Models outside any evidenced family remain unknown. Such configurations stay in the coverage table and research queue. Source dates and methods are inspectable, and **Include estimates** can be disabled.
